  "sourceSolutions": [
    {
      "source": "1070",
      "task": "Determine the smallest worst-case trial guarantee for identifying the full lock/key correspondence under a bijection model.",
      "solution": "Assume every key opens exactly one of the six locks, every lock has one key, and a trial tests one chosen pair with success/failure. For one lock test at most five keys; after five failures infer the remaining key without testing it. Remove its key and lock. Continue at most4,3,2,1 trials for subsequent locks; the final pair is inferred. The bound is5+4+3+2+1=15. Lower bound: an adversary replies failure whenever at least one still-possible bijection survives failure; if an edge is forced in every remaining bijection its positive answer is redundant information. At final unique bijection, each of15 unordered lock pairs admits a swapped-key alternative unless at least one of its two cross edges was tested negative. The cross edges belonging to different unordered pairs are disjoint. Thus at least15 negative trials are required in this consistent worst-case path. Therefore15 is the minimum guarantee, not a claim that every instance needs15.",
      "sourceQualification": "Original prose does not separately state bijection, one-key/one-lock trial model, inferred-match allowance, or worst-case minimum. These must be explicit qualified conditions, not recovered original facts."
    },
    {
      "source": "1071",
      "task": "Find a guaranteed weighing count to sort four distinct masses and prove minimality in the unknown-mass balance model.",
      "solution": "CompareA withB,thenC withD,ordering each pair. Compare their heavier members; relabel so A>B and C>D and A>C. A is greatest. CompareB withC: ifB>C,orderA>B>C>D; otherwise compareB withD to obtainA>C>B>D orA>C>D>B. At most5 pairwise weighings suffice. Lower bound applies even if groups of balls may be put in the pans: consider all24 assignments of masses1,2,4,8 to the four labeled balls. Distinct disjoint nonempty subsets have different sums, so a useful balance weighing has only left-heavy/right-heavy outcomes in these inputs. Four weighings yield at most16 decision leaves, insufficient to identify24 orders. Hence any universal method needs at least5 in the worst case. This lower bound does not assume an unprovided equality outcome is impossible for every imaginable mass tuple; it only selects a valid family on which equality never supplies information.",
      "sourceQualification": "This answers the minimal worst-case guarantee for arbitrary distinct positive masses. The original does not explicitly call it a minimum or exclude group weighings; the proof covers group-weighing algorithms as well."
    },
    {
      "source": "1072",
      "task": "Determine the complete student/grade assignment with precisely one incorrect prediction.",
      "solution": "The grades are a permutation of3,4,5. IfAlex gets5 andSam gets3,thenBen gets4 and all three predictions are true,disallowed. IfAlex gets5 andSam gets4,thenBen gets3;Alex's5 prediction andBen's not5 prediction are true,Sam's3 prediction false,so this works. The other four assignments(Alex,Sam,Ben)=(3,4,5),(3,5,4),(4,3,5),(4,5,3) have respectively0,1,1,1 true predictions,not2. Thus uniquelyAlex5,Sam4,Ben3. No grade,student or negation is changed."
    },
    {
      "source": "1073",
      "task": "Give a winning strategy and identify which player wins under correct play.",
      "solution": "Using the stated first ten integers1..10, the first player starts at1. If the opponent adds r in1..10,the first player adds11-r,also in1..10. The first player's spoken totals become1,12,23,34,45,56,67,78,89,100. Thus the first player reaches100. The opponent can never skip100 in this strategy: before the final reply the last controlled total is89,so the opponent only reaches90..99. Earlier opponent totals are strictly below100 as well. Therefore no unstated ban on overshooting is needed to establish this guaranteed winning strategy. The necessary increment r is inferable from the two announced totals even if initially chosen privately.",
      "sourceQualification": "The 'first ten numbers' is read as the ten listed positive integers; an arbitrary real increment continuum would be a different model. The proof does not import a rule forbidding totals above100."
    },
    {
      "source": "1074",
      "task": "Identify the winner of the adjacent right/up/diagonal corner game and give a guarantee on a standard chessboard.",
      "solution": "On a standard8x8 board give squares coordinates0..7 on each axis,starting(0,0),target(7,7). First player moves diagonally to(1,1). Thereafter repeat the opponent's previous direction once: right,right;up,up;or diagonal,diagonal as a two-move pair. Every position after the first player's move has both coordinates odd. At least one coordinate after an opponent move is even,so that player cannot occupy(7,7). Copying is always legal: any coordinate changed by an opponent move began odd<=5,so it becomes even<=6 and the copied move becomes odd<=7. Finite monotone moves end with the first player reaching(7,7). Equivalently positions with both remaining distances to target even are losing for the next player.",
      "sourceQualification": "Source says chessboard but not an explicit size.8x8 is the standard chessboard convention; a qualified visible task must state size and adjacent one-square steps. The original does not reference a diagram."
    },
    {
      "source": "1078",
      "task": "Determine every participant/master count under explicit one-game-per-pair chess scoring, without inferring sporting dominance from titles.",
      "solution": "Assume a single round robin with each pair playing once,win1/draw1/2/loss0. Let f first-class players,m masters,n=f+m. Each first-class player's points versus their own class equal half their total; summing makes first-class cross-class points f(f-1)/2, equal to all within-class points. Similarly each master's points versus first-class players equal all their within-master points,m(m-1)/2. Every cross-class game contributes1 total point,so fm=[f(f-1)+m(m-1)]/2. Hence(f-m)^2=f+m=n. With9<n<25,the only integer square is16;|f-m|=4,so(f,m)=(10,6) or(6,10). Both are possible. For f10,m6,draw every within-class game;pair the ten first-class players into five pairs,one in each pair wins against masters0,1,2 and draws against3,4,5,the other wins against3,4,5 and draws against0,1,2. Each first-class player scores4.5 both within and across classes;each master scores2.5 within and across classes. For f6,m10,interchange the class roles in that construction. Thus total16 with masters6 or10,not a unique master count from the conditions. A higher title does not state that a participant must beat a lower title.",
      "sourceQualification": "Original prose does not specify round-robin completeness or win/draw points. Without a schedule/scoring contract these count equations are not forced. Preserve this qualification; do not add a dominance assumption to select one master count."
    },
    {
      "source": "1086",
      "task": "Produce four nonconstant factors for x^8+x^4-1 in an explicitly allowed real-coefficient factorization.",
      "solution": "Let alpha=(1+sqrt5)/2,beta=(sqrt5-1)/2,so alpha-beta=1,alpha*beta=1,alpha,beta>0. Put s=sqrt(alpha),t=sqrt(beta),h=sqrt(2s). Then(x^2+h*x+s)(x^2-h*x+s)=x^4+alpha because2s-h^2=0,s^2=alpha. Also(x^2+t)(x^2-t)=x^4-beta. Product of these four nonconstant quadratics is(x^4+alpha)(x^4-beta)=x^8+x^4-1. Every factor containsx;no constant factor is being counted.",
      "sourceQualification": "Original request does not name the allowed coefficient field. The constructed four-factor result is over real coefficients. It does not claim a rational/integer-coefficient decomposition or recover an explicit original field restriction."
    },
    {
      "source": "1088",
      "task": "Determine the parameter with root-square sum9 and preserve the real-root domain qualification.",
      "solution": "For rootsr,s,r+s=1-a andrs=-2a. Thusr^2+s^2=(1-a)^2+4a=(a+1)^2=9,yieldinga=2 or-4 algebraically. Under real-root interpretation discriminanta^2+6a+1 must be nonnegative. Ata2 it is17>0 and both roots are real;ata-4 it is-7<0,so real-root solution is onlya2. If complex roots are allowed,a-4 also gives squared-root sum9 (roots(5+/-i*sqrt7)/2). The source does not explicitly state complex roots,so do not silently claim the formal second root is a real admissible one.",
      "sourceQualification": "Choice view must explicitly name real roots or present the two domains conditionally. No unprovided root-field convention is claimed recovered."
    }
